  "hypothesis": "H88-PHYS-NEAR: Geothermal fault damage zones and secondary strands show coupled radiometric potassium enrichment + magnetic demagnetization troughs + topographic lineament. Using this multi-physics conjunction to weight dot emission within 3-25 px of catalogue faults (where holdout can score withheld-segment recovery), plus a true blind-zone component (>26 px), differs from all prior lanes that use distance-alone or lattice placement. All features from visible catalogue + official external data (USGS GeoDAWN radiometrics, training_features RTP/DEM, SGMC faults).",
  "mechanism": "Hydrothermal alteration creates K enrichment / Th depletion (GeoDAWN rad_K_over_Th anomaly), magnetite destruction creates magnetic demagnetization troughs (negative RTP residual from training_features band 2), fault scarps create topographic lineaments (DEM curvature contrast from bands 12 and 19). Proximal halo (3-25 px) targets secondary structures near known faults for holdout recovery; blind zone (>26 px) targets truly novel faults. SGMC concealed-fault clearance (>1 km) and volcanic-vent masking prevent double-counting.",
  "named_non_fault_process": "Pleistocene pluvial shoreline wave-cut benches (Lake Lahontan ~1332 m, Lake Bonneville ~1463/1552 m) create linear topographic features without radiometric or magnetic anomalies; closed-basin playas produce flat lows without K/Th enrichment; alluvial fan apices create radial drainage patterns without fault geometry.",
